#X obj 674 8 convolve~;
#X obj 230 78 cnv 1 535 651 empty empty empty 17 7 0 10 -237178 -1 0;
#X text 240 83 Real-time FFT convolution. Multiplies the incoming audio
signal by the impulse response (IR) loaded into the object \, using
fast FFT-based convolution. The result is a signal that sounds as if
the input were played through the acoustic space \, hardware or filter
described by the IR. The maximum IR size and normalization are controlled
by the @maxsize and @norm properties.;
#X text 240 199 Syntax: load the IR with one of the methods (load_file
\, load_array \, set) \, then send the signal to the audio inlet. The
object outputs the convolved signal from its audio outlet.;
#X text 240 269 Typical use cases:;
#X text 250 294 • Reverb — applying a real acoustic space to a dry
signal by convolving it with an impulse response recorded in a concert
hall \, church \, studio or other venue.;
#X text 250 349 • Cabinet and microphone simulation — convolving a
guitar or synthesizer signal with an impulse response of a speaker
cabinet \, microphone or preamp to reproduce its characteristic tone.;
#X text 250 419 • Hardware and analog emulation — reproducing the sound
of vintage hardware (tape machines \, plate reverbs \, spring reverbs
\, equalizers) by convolving the signal with a measured impulse response
of that device.;
#X text 250 489 • Creative sound design — using short or unusual impulse
responses (metallic hits \, footsteps \, noise bursts \, reversed recordings)
to obtain original textures and resonances that go far beyond natural
acoustics.;
#X text 250 559 • Filtering with measured responses — applying the
measured frequency response of a physical object (a violin body \,
a pipe \, a room) as a filter to a synthetic signal.;
#X text 240 629 Note: real-time FFT convolution is CPU-intensive. Larger
impulse responses (above a few seconds) require more processing power
\, so it is recommended to keep @maxsize at a reasonable value and
to use shorter IRs when working with several instances at once.;
